---
title: Hawaiian Rolls
image: https://handletheheat.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/06/hawaiian-bread-rolls-SQUARE.jpg
course: Side Dish
prep time: 35 minutes
source: https://handletheheat.com/homemade-hawaiian-bread-rolls/
cuisine: American
time required: 3 hours 55 minutes
servings: 15
author: Tessa Arias
cook time: 20 minutes
---

Combine @pineapple juice{1/2%cup}(canned, at room temperature, about 116 grams) , @milk{1/2%cup}(warmed to 100-110°F, about 116 grams), @butter{5%tablespoons}(unsalted, melted and slightly cooled to just warm, ~85°F), @sugar{6%tablespoons}, @brown sugar{2%tablespoons}(light), @eggs{2}(room temperature), @vanilla extract{1%teaspoon}, and @instant yeast{2.25%teaspoons}(about 1 package) in the bowl of a #stand mixer{}. 

> If you don't have instant yeast, you can use active dry yeast instead. It will result in 20% more time to rise though, or combine with the warm milk to proof for 5 minutes before adding into the bowl.

Add @all-purpose flour{1/4%cup}(about 32 grams), plus @bread flour{2%cups}(about 286 grams), and stir with a wooden spoon until the dough forms a rough, shaggy mass. 

> The mix of bread to all-purpose flour is essential. Just all-purpose flour makes dense, drier rolls, with longer kneading and rising time. Only bread flour makes too firm of rolls.

Stir in @salt{1.5%teaspoons}. 

Attach the dough hook to the mixer and turn to medium-low speed. Gradually add @bread flour{2%cups}(about 286 grams), then continue to slowly add just enough @?bread flour{1/4%cup} for the dough to come together.

> It's better to be slightly sticky than dense. This dough shouldn't be unbearably sticky, but it will be tacky. Resist the urge to add extra flour as this will negatively impact the texture of the rolls. The dough will be easier to work with after the first rise.

Continue kneading on medium-high speed for ~{4-5%minutes}, until a soft and smooth ball of dough forms.

Lightly grease or wet your hands and place the dough in a lightly greased medium-sized bowl. Cover with plastic wrap. Let rise until puffy and doubled in size, about ~{1.5%hours}.

Spray a #9x13-inch metal baking pan{} with cooking spray. Gently deflate the dough. Use a bench scraper or knife to divide the dough into 15 equal pieces. Shape each piece into a ball and place in the prepared pan.

In a small bowl, combine @egg{1}(lightly beaten) with @water{2%tablespoons}. Brush all over the rolls. Cover with plastic wrap and let rise again until doubled in size, about ~{1%hour}.

Meanwhile, preheat the oven to 375°F.

Bake the rolls for ~{20%minutes}, or until golden brown. Brush with @butter{1%tablespoon}(salted, melted). 

> Store leftovers in an airtight container for up to 3 days. Don't refrigerate, as it will dry them out faster. Refresh in a 300°F oven for 5 minutes, or pop in the microwave for 15-20 seconds, or until warm, before serving.

> To freeze Hawaiian Rolls, once cooled, place rolls in a zip-top bag or airtight container and freeze for up to 3 months. To thaw, leave at room temperature for a few hours. Reheat in a 400°F oven for 5-10 minutes, or pop in the microwave for 15-20 seconds, until warmed through, if desired. 

> An additional recipe to compare: https://www.ihearteating.com/hawaiian-sweet-rolls/